1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,674
How can one country have eleven different

2
00:00:02,674 --> 00:00:03,392
time zones?

3
00:00:03,742 --> 00:00:06,302
This is Russia, the biggest country on

4
00:00:06,302 --> 00:00:09,468
Earth. It covers about seventeen million square

5
00:00:09,468 --> 00:00:12,027
kilometers, more than one ninth of all

6
00:00:12,027 --> 00:00:13,577
the land on our planet.

7
00:00:13,927 --> 00:00:16,411
It stretches from Europe all the way

8
00:00:16,411 --> 00:00:18,895
to the Pacific Ocean. That's so wide

9
00:00:18,895 --> 00:00:20,965
that the Sun rises in the east

10
00:00:20,965 --> 00:00:23,311
about ten hours before it rises in

11
00:00:23,311 --> 00:00:23,932
the west.

12
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:26,607
So when someone in Moscow is eating

13
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:29,662
dinner, someone in Kamchatka is already waking

14
00:00:29,662 --> 00:00:30,725
up for tomorrow.

15
00:00:31,075 --> 00:00:34,306
The westernmost time zone isn't even connected

16
00:00:34,306 --> 00:00:36,905
to the rest of Russia. Kaliningrad is

17
00:00:36,905 --> 00:00:39,786
a piece of Russia squeezed between Poland

18
00:00:39,786 --> 00:00:42,104
and Lithuania, on the Baltic Sea.

19
00:00:42,454 --> 00:00:45,856
The famous Trans-Siberian Railway crosses most of

20
00:00:45,856 --> 00:00:48,910
the country, from Moscow to Vladivostok. The

21
00:00:48,910 --> 00:00:51,201
full trip takes about a week, and

22
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:53,770
you change your watch again and again

23
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:54,742
along the way.

24
00:00:55,092 --> 00:00:57,608
Most of that land is Siberia: huge

25
00:00:57,608 --> 00:01:00,790
forests, frozen ground and very few people.

26
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:03,676
Most Russians live in the western part,

27
00:01:03,676 --> 00:01:04,564
near Europe.

28
00:01:04,914 --> 00:01:07,851
Fun fact: France technically has even more

29
00:01:07,851 --> 00:01:10,788
time zones, twelve, because of its islands

30
00:01:10,788 --> 00:01:13,515
spread around the world. But Russia has

31
00:01:13,515 --> 00:01:15,892
the most on one connected piece of

32
00:01:15,892 --> 00:01:16,242
land.

33
00:01:16,592 --> 00:01:18,845
Here's how it works in daily life.

34
00:01:18,845 --> 00:01:21,363
When it's noon in Moscow, it's already

35
00:01:21,363 --> 00:01:24,079
nine at night on the Kamchatka Peninsula.

36
00:01:24,079 --> 00:01:25,934
A phone call from one end of

37
00:01:25,934 --> 00:01:28,187
the country to the other can cross

38
00:01:28,187 --> 00:01:29,115
most of a day.

39
00:01:29,465 --> 00:01:32,386
Russia even changed its mind about time

40
00:01:32,386 --> 00:01:34,708
zones. In 2010 it cut them down

41
00:01:34,708 --> 00:01:36,880
to nine, then went back up to

42
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,876
eleven a few years later, because people

43
00:01:39,876 --> 00:01:42,498
complained it was dark at the wrong

44
00:01:42,498 --> 00:01:42,947
times.

45
00:01:43,297 --> 00:01:45,581
Russia is so wide that it borders

46
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:48,558
fourteen countries, from Norway in the west

47
00:01:48,558 --> 00:01:50,703
to North Korea in the east, and

48
00:01:50,703 --> 00:01:53,472
across the Bering Strait, Alaska is less

49
00:01:53,472 --> 00:01:55,756
than one hundred kilometers away.

50
00:01:56,106 --> 00:01:58,432
In the middle of the Bering Strait

51
00:01:58,432 --> 00:02:01,032
sit two small islands: Big Diomede, in

52
00:02:01,032 --> 00:02:03,837
Russia, and Little Diomede, in the United

53
00:02:03,837 --> 00:02:06,779
States. They are only about four kilometers

54
00:02:06,779 --> 00:02:09,926
apart, but because the International Date Line

55
00:02:09,926 --> 00:02:12,388
runs between them, they are almost a

56
00:02:12,388 --> 00:02:13,962
full day apart in time.

57
00:02:14,312 --> 00:02:16,573
Would you ever take the week-long train

58
00:02:16,573 --> 00:02:18,835
across Russia? Tell me in the comments.

